    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Math anxiety is extremely common in the United States, where one frequently hears “I’m just not a math person” whenever the topic comes up. Often, negative early math experiences cause students to perceive themselves this way, and once that impression is made, it’s hard to overcome. Significantly, mathematical performance at the beginning of kindergarten is predictive of performance later in elementary, middle, and high school, and it’s not a stretch to think that students who come to kindergarten unprepared mathematically are more likely to develop math anxiety.\r\n\r\nIn order to be ready for kindergarten at age 5, preschoolers need to learn a number of early numeracy skills, such as counting to 20 and demonstrating one-to-one correspondence when counting. Both conventional wisdom and research suggest that young children learn best interacting with an adult, but the full attention of an adult is not always available. Tablets and apps present an intriguing opportunity to fill this gap, but research suggests that young children have trouble transferring knowledge learned about 2-D objects on a screen to their 3-D real world counterparts.\r\n\r\nPolymath attempts to address this “transfer deficit” by combining physical objects with a tablet app. Learners build shapes on the surface of a tablet by connecting cubes together magnetically. They are then prompted to count how many blocks they used, while being given feedback based on their response.\r\n\r\nDuring the design of Polymath, four major rounds of user testing were conducted with Tommy, a 3.5-year-old preschooler. A qualitative analysis of the level of scaffolding necessary for Tommy to complete the mathematical tasks suggested that they were appropriate for a child of his level, and video observation demonstrates that Tommy learned from the feedback that Polymath provided.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
